  "plain_language_conclusion": "Sensitivity analysis reveals that estimated transit durations scale linearly and predictably with proportional adjustments to multiplier constants (e.g., +/-25% shifts in mode factor or overall road hierarchy shift travel times by exactly +/-25.0%, while +/-25% shifts in slope or V90 alter travel times by approximately +/-12.8% and -9.8%/+14.8% respectively). However, topological route selection exhibits absolute insensitivity: 0.0% of the 1,200 evaluated routes changed across all +/-25% parameter perturbations. This dichotomy occurs because the stylized 23-node graph offers sparse alternative cycles whose geometric detours are roughly 50% longer than direct paths, requiring extreme multiplier differentials far beyond +/-25% to trigger route divergence. Readers must note that these constants are heuristic, uncalibrated engineering assumptions, and actual travel times must not be interpreted as empirical measurements on Hyderabad corridors."
